--TEST--
`Core\Response::redirect` names both sides of what a `Location` line carries, and declares nothing when it refuses -- spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound is the header line's, not the URL grammar's: a field value is
// printable ASCII and never empty. The newline is the one that matters -- a
// destination carrying one would end the header line and begin a second the
// program never wrote -- and the empty string is refused because a `Location`
// naming nothing is a redirect to nowhere.
array<string> $unwritable = ["", "/next\r\nX-Injected: 1", "/next\nSet-Cookie: a=b", "/next\tspaced"];
uint $refused = 0;
foreach ($unwritable as string $where) {
    try {
        Core\Response::redirect($where);
    } catch (LogicError $unspellable) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of 4\n";

// The other side of the same bound, one byte in from each end of the printable
// range: a member that stopped one value early would still look right against
// the half above.
array<string> $accepted = [" ", "~", "/a b", "/next~"];
uint $carried = 0;
foreach ($accepted as string $edge) {
    try {
        Core\Response::redirect($edge);
        $carried = $carried + 1;
    } catch (LogicError $rejected) {
    }
}
echo "carried ", $carried, " of 4\n";

// A refusal leaves nothing behind, on `setHeader`'s rule: both checks run
// before either declaration, so neither the status nor the header is written
// for a call that threw -- and no byte of body is either.
var $onRefusal = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Core\Response::redirect("/next\r\nX-Injected: 1");
    } catch (LogicError $unwritten) {
    }
});
echo "a refusal wrote [", $onRefusal, "]\n";

// And the sentence says which rule refused, since a URL and a header value are
// two grammars a program could have had in mind.
try {
    Core\Response::redirect("");
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused 4 of 4
carried 4 of 4
a refusal wrote []
Core\Response::redirect(): `` is not a destination a `Location` header can carry — a field value is printable ASCII and never empty, so an empty URL and a newline that would begin a second header are refused alike
